<h2>Renderings: appearance and atmosphere</h2>
<p>Renderings communicate materials, colors, lighting, landscape, human activity, and the intended experience from a selected viewpoint.</p>
<p>They help an audience imagine what a space may look and feel like, but they show the project through a camera position chosen by the creator.</p>

<h2>Animation: sequence and narrative</h2>
<p>Animation guides the audience through a planned series of views.</p>
<p>It can explain movement, circulation, construction, assembly, or changes over time. It is effective for telling a clear story, but the audience follows a predetermined route.</p>

<h2>AR: design in an existing environment</h2>
<p>Augmented reality places digital content over a view of the physical world through a phone, tablet, or headset.</p>
<p>It can help audiences explore placement, approximate scale, alternative configurations, and design information within an existing site or space.</p>

<h2>VR: immersive spatial experience</h2>
<p>Virtual reality places the viewer inside a digital environment at an apparent full scale.</p>
<p>It can help communicate interiors, sightlines, circulation, and spatial experience. However, it usually requires dedicated equipment and is often experienced individually.</p>

<h2>Physical models: shared spatial understanding</h2>
<p>A physical model gives the audience a tangible object that can be examined together from multiple viewpoints.</p>
<p>It is particularly effective for communicating:</p>
<ul>
<li>Overall form and proportion</li>
<li>Building massing</li>
<li>Site and urban context</li>
<li>Circulation and access</li>
<li>Relationships between buildings and open space</li>
<li>Topography and landscape</li>
<li>Multiple viewpoints at once</li>
</ul>
<p>People can gather around the model, point to the same relationships, and discuss what they see without specialist software or equipment.</p>

<h2>Which medium should you use?</h2>
<p>Use a rendering when the priority is visual appearance and atmosphere.</p>
<p>Use animation when the priority is sequence, movement, or a controlled narrative.</p>
<p>Use AR when the priority is placing digital information within an existing physical environment.</p>
<p>Use VR when the priority is an immersive, full-scale spatial experience.</p>
<p>Use a physical model when the priority is shared understanding of scale, form, context, and spatial relationships.</p>

<h2>When should you combine them?</h2>
<p>Many projects benefit from more than one medium.</p>
<p>A physical model can establish the overall project and its relationships. Renderings can show material character and atmosphere. Animation can explain circulation or sequence. AR can connect a proposal to an existing environment, while VR can provide an immersive experience of particular spaces.</p>
<p>The best combination is determined by what the audience needs to understand—not by using every available technology.</p>

<h2>Coordinating physical and digital deliverables</h2>
<p>Carve's core deliverable is the physical model. When a project benefits from additional communication formats, animation, AR, VR, or other digital presentation materials can be separately scoped and coordinated alongside it.</p>
<p>Requirements and availability depend on the intended audience, source information, platform, and decision the presentation needs to support.</p>

<h2>Begin with the audience and decision</h2>
<p>Before selecting a communication medium, ask:</p>
<ul>
<li>Who is the audience?</li>
<li>What do they need to understand?</li>
<li>What information is difficult to interpret?</li>
<li>Will they experience the presentation individually or together?</li>
<li>What decision must the presentation support?</li>
</ul>
<p>The answers determine whether the project needs a physical model, a digital presentation, or a coordinated combination.</p>
